M="biochem-molecular-1"
SUB="Molecular"
T={
"chrom":[("The core of a nucleosome is formed by two molecules each of which histones?",["H2A, H2B, H3 and H4","H1, H2A, H2B and H3","H1, H2B, H3 and H4","H1, H2A, H3 and H4"],0,"Histone H1 is not part of the core; it binds the linker DNA between nucleosomes."),
 ("Histones bind DNA tightly because they are rich in:",["Lysine and arginine","Aspartate and glutamate","Cysteine and methionine","Serine and threonine"],0,"These residues carry positive charges that attract the negatively charged phosphate backbone."),
 ("Actively transcribed genes are found mainly in:",["Euchromatin","Heterochromatin","Centromeric DNA","Telomeric DNA"],0,"The relaxed form allows the transcription machinery to reach the DNA.",2)],
"epig":[("Histone acetylation promotes transcription because it:",["Removes positive charge from lysine","Adds negative charge to the DNA","Breaks the DNA into open loops","Recruits the ribosome to the gene"],0,"Weaker histone-DNA attraction loosens the nucleosome and opens access."),
 ("The methyl donor for DNA methylation is:",["S-adenosylmethionine","Tetrahydrofolate","Methylcobalamin","Betaine"],0,"Methyltransferases transfer the methyl group from SAM to cytosine."),
 ("A tumour suppressor gene is intact on sequencing but its protein is absent. Methylation of CpG islands is found in its promoter. This silencing is best described as:",["Epigenetic","A missense change","A frameshift change","Loss of heterozygosity"],0,"The base sequence is unchanged; only which genes are read has changed, and that state is passed to daughter cells.",3)],
"purine":[("Purine synthesis differs from pyrimidine synthesis in that the purine ring is:",["Built directly onto the sugar","Completed before the sugar is added","Assembled entirely in mitochondria","Derived from a preformed ring"],0,"Pyrimidines complete the ring first and attach PRPP afterwards."),
 ("The committed step of de novo purine synthesis is catalysed by:",["Glutamine-PRPP amidotransferase","Phosphoribosylpyrophosphate synthetase","Ribonucleotide reductase","Adenylosuccinate lyase"],0,"It is inhibited by the AMP and GMP it eventually produces."),
 ("Why do antifolate drugs impair purine synthesis?",["Tetrahydrofolate donates two carbons","Folate is a precursor of the ring","Folate activates the committed step","Folate carries the amide nitrogen"],0,"Two separate steps of ring assembly need a one-carbon unit from THF.",2)],
"pyrim":[("Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase II differs from CPS I in that it:",["Uses glutamine in the cytosol","Uses ammonia in the cytosol","Uses glutamine in mitochondria","Uses aspartate in mitochondria"],0,"CPS I is mitochondrial, uses ammonia and belongs to the urea cycle."),
 ("Ribonucleotide reductase is required for which purpose?",["Making deoxyribonucleotides","Making ribonucleotides","Removing the pyrimidine ring","Salvaging purine bases"],0,"It acts in S phase and keeps the four dNTP pools in balance."),
 ("Which enzyme is the direct target of 5-fluorouracil?",["Thymidylate synthase","Dihydrofolate reductase","Ribonucleotide reductase","Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase II"],0,"Blocking it starves the cell of dTMP, producing so-called thymineless death.",2)],
"nucdrug":[("Hydroxyurea acts by inhibiting:",["Ribonucleotide reductase","Thymidylate synthase","Dihydrofolate reductase","Xanthine oxidase"],0,"All four deoxyribonucleotides fall, so DNA synthesis stops."),
 ("Leucovorin rescues cells from methotrexate because it:",["Bypasses dihydrofolate reductase","Displaces methotrexate from DNA","Inhibits methotrexate transport","Speeds renal methotrexate clearance"],0,"It is already a reduced folate and needs no DHFR step."),
 ("A patient on azathioprine is started on allopurinol for gout and develops severe pancytopenia. The reason is that allopurinol:",["Blocks a main route of azathioprine breakdown","Increases azathioprine absorption","Displaces azathioprine from protein","Reduces renal azathioprine excretion"],0,"Xanthine oxidase is a major catabolic pathway for these thiopurines, so the dose must be cut substantially.",3)],
"salvage":[("Lesch-Nyhan syndrome results from near-complete deficiency of:",["HGPRT","Adenosine deaminase","Xanthine oxidase","Adenosine kinase"],0,"Hypoxanthine and guanine cannot be salvaged, so they are degraded to uric acid."),
 ("Why does de novo purine synthesis increase in Lesch-Nyhan syndrome?",["PRPP accumulates and inhibitors fall","The committed enzyme is itself mutated","Uric acid stimulates the pathway","Salvaged bases activate the pathway"],0,"More substrate plus less feedback inhibition drives the committed step harder."),
 ("In adenosine deaminase deficiency, lymphocytes die because accumulated dATP:",["Inhibits ribonucleotide reductase","Inhibits DNA polymerase directly","Breaks the DNA strands","Blocks the salvage pathway"],0,"With that enzyme shut down no deoxyribonucleotide can be made, so no cell can replicate its DNA.",3)],
"repl":[("DNA polymerase requires a primer because it cannot:",["Begin a new strand by itself","Read a template strand","Work in the 5' to 3' direction","Separate the parental strands"],0,"Primase lays down a short RNA primer to give it a 3' end to extend."),
 ("The lagging strand is synthesised discontinuously because polymerase works only:",["From 5' to 3'","From 3' to 5'","Away from the fork","On the leading template"],0,"The two template strands run in opposite directions, so one must be copied in short pieces."),
 ("Which enzyme relieves the supercoiling that builds up ahead of the replication fork?",["Topoisomerase","DNA helicase","DNA ligase","DNA primase"],0,"Type I cuts one strand and type II cuts both, which is why type II is the target of fluoroquinolones and etoposide.",2)],
"replenz":[("Proofreading during replication uses which activity?",["3' to 5' exonuclease","5' to 3' exonuclease","Endonuclease","Helicase"],0,"Each newly added nucleotide is checked against the template and excised if wrong."),
 ("In prokaryotes the RNA primer is removed by:",["DNA polymerase I","DNA polymerase III","DNA ligase","Primase"],0,"It uses its 5' to 3' exonuclease activity and fills the gap with DNA."),
 ("Replication fidelity is far higher than the intrinsic error rate of the polymerase alone because of:",["Proofreading plus mismatch repair","A slower rate of chain growth","A second round of replication","Methylation of the newly made strand"],0,"Three layers act in series: enzyme specificity, exonuclease proofreading, and mismatch repair afterwards.",3)],
"telo":[("Telomerase carries which two components?",["A reverse transcriptase and an RNA template","Two reverse transcriptase subunits and a ligase","A DNA template and a ligase","A helicase and a primase"],0,"The RNA base-pairs with the single-stranded 3' end and is copied into DNA."),
 ("When telomeres shorten past a critical length, the cell:",["Can no longer divide","Undergoes immediate lysis","Reverts to a stem cell state","Loses its nuclear membrane"],0,"This state is called senescence."),
 ("A tumour cell line divides indefinitely in culture without telomere shortening. Which change best explains this?",["Reactivation of telomerase","Loss of a mismatch repair gene","Gain of extra centromeres","Failure of nucleotide excision repair"],0,"Telomere length acts as a mitotic clock, and restoring telomerase removes the limit on division number.",3)],
"repair":[("Xeroderma pigmentosum is caused by a defect in:",["Nucleotide excision repair","Base excision repair","Mismatch repair","Nonhomologous end joining"],0,"Pyrimidine dimers from UV light cannot be removed, so skin cancers appear early."),
 ("Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer results from defective:",["Mismatch repair","Nucleotide excision repair","Base excision repair","Homologous recombination"],0,"It accounts for only about 5 percent of all colorectal cancer."),
 ("Homologous recombination is more accurate than nonhomologous end joining because it:",["Copies from an intact template","Acts earlier in the cell cycle","Removes a longer stretch of DNA","Needs no ligation step"],0,"End joining simply fuses the broken ends and usually loses bases at the junction.",2)],
"mut":[("A base change that still specifies the same amino acid is called:",["Silent","Missense","Nonsense","Frameshift"],0,"It usually occurs at the third position of a codon."),
 ("Deletion of two nucleotides from a coding region produces:",["A frameshift mutation","A silent mutation","A missense mutation","A splice site mutation"],0,"Only insertions or deletions in multiples of three preserve the reading frame."),
 ("A boy has severe progressive weakness from early childhood, while his cousin with a mutation in the same gene walks into adulthood. The difference is best explained by:",["One mutation shifts the reading frame","One mutation is at a splice site","One gene has twice as many copies","One mutation is silent"],0,"Duchenne usually arises from an out-of-frame mutation, whereas Becker comes from an in-frame one that still yields partly functional protein.",3)],
}

BOSS=dict(
 th="เคสใหญ่: เด็กชายผิวไหม้ง่ายและมีก้อนที่ใบหน้า",
 en="Boss case: a boy with sun sensitivity and facial lesions",
 case="เด็กชายอายุ 6 ปี มาด้วยผิวไหม้รุนแรงทุกครั้งที่ออกแดดแม้ช่วงสั้น ๆ ตั้งแต่อายุ 1 ปี ตรวจพบกระจำนวนมากบนใบหน้าและแขน พร้อมก้อนแข็งสองก้อนที่จมูกและโหนกแก้ม ผลชิ้นเนื้อเป็น basal cell carcinoma ทั้งสองก้อน พี่สาวเสียชีวิตจากมะเร็งผิวหนังเมื่ออายุ 11 ปี พ่อแม่เป็นลูกพี่ลูกน้องกัน",
 qs=[("Which type of DNA damage is this boy unable to repair?",["Pyrimidine dimers","Mismatched base pairs","Double-strand breaks","Deaminated cytosine"],0,"Ultraviolet light cross-links adjacent pyrimidines, which distorts the helix."),
  ("The repair pathway that is defective in this disease is:",["Nucleotide excision repair","Base excision repair","Mismatch repair","Homologous recombination"],0,"It excises a short oligonucleotide containing the lesion and fills the gap."),
  ("The parents being first cousins makes which inheritance pattern most likely?",["Autosomal recessive","Autosomal dominant","X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial"],0,"Consanguinity raises the chance that both parents carry the same rare allele."),
  ("Which protein normally cleaves the damaged strand on both sides of the lesion?",["An excision endonuclease","A DNA ligase","A DNA helicase","A DNA methyltransferase"],0,"Cutting on both the 5' and 3' sides releases the damaged segment as one piece."),
  ("After the damaged segment is removed, which two enzymes complete the repair?",["DNA polymerase then ligase","Ligase then DNA polymerase","Primase then polymerase","Helicase then ligase"],0,"The gap is filled by polymerase using the intact strand, and ligase seals the final nick."),
  ("If instead this boy had presented at age 45 with colonic adenocarcinoma and a strong family history of early colon and endometrial cancer, the defective pathway would be:",["Mismatch repair","Nucleotide excision repair","Base excision repair","Nonhomologous end joining"],0,"That is Lynch syndrome, in which mismatch repair fails; it accounts for roughly 5 percent of colorectal cancer overall.")],
 pass_=5)
